+## Source Page Audit
+
+SOURCE-READ.  I rendered Moh PDF pages 149, 151, 179-181, and 207-209 as page
+images under `box/k16t2-gate-20260903/` and inspected the relevant formulas.
+
+SOURCE-READ (Moh p.149).  Theorem 1.2 assumes a monic `f`, a coherent complete
+system of pi-roots with multiplicities and accuracy `lambda`, an integer
+`d | gcd(l_i)`, and a `d`-th quasi-approximate root `h`.  Moh writes the
+expansion
+
+```text
+f(y) = h(y)^d + sum_{j=1}^d h_j(y) h(y)^(d-j),
+deg h_j < deg h = deg f / d,
+ord h_j(sigma_i) >= (lambda/d) j.
+```
+
+Thus Theorem 1.2 is an order theorem for coefficients in an approximate-root
+expansion.  It does not itself impose a final finite support table, a particular
+Newton polygon beyond what is supplied by the chosen coherent roots, or the
+tuple-level Laurent bridge.
+
+SOURCE-READ (Moh p.151).  Lemma 2.1 is stated for constant Jacobian.  In Moh's
+root parameter `g=eta^(-n)`,
+
+```text
+f=eta^(-m)+sum_{i>-m} f_i(x) eta^i,
+ord_eta(sum f_i(x)eta^i - sum f_i(0)eta^i) = n-1,
+deg_x f_(n-1)(x) = 1.
+```
+
+The proof gives `f_i'(x)=0` for `i<n-1` and `f_(n-1)'(x)` a nonzero constant.
+For `J=c gamma`, the same chain-rule comparison shifts the anchor degree to
+`deg_gamma = 2`.  Crucially, this is a Laurent statement in the root parameter
+of the other polynomial; it is not an ordinary coefficient condition in the
+`pi`-polynomial chart.
+
+SOURCE-READ (Moh p.208).  Moh's `(16,12;13;3;X)` control uses Theorem 1.2 in
+exactly this form: a common `h`, an `h`-expansion for both polynomials, order
+inequalities at the two displayed roots, then a reduced support chart in
+`A`, `B`, and `y-x`.  This supports the charged method as a chart-building
+pattern, not as a printed standard-basis certificate for the K16 `t=2` case.
+
+## Safe Normalizations
+
+PROVED-HERE / AUDITED.  The normalizations used by the chart are automorphisms
+or choices of approximate-root coordinate; they are not case restrictions.
+
+1. Source shear to put one tangent at `pi=0`.  If the two top directions are
+   `pi=r1 gamma` and `pi=r2 gamma`, use
+   `(gamma,pi) -> (gamma, pi-r1 gamma)`.  This is a triangular automorphism of
+   `k[gamma,pi]`, keeps `pi`-degree and monicity because the coefficient of the
+   highest power of `pi` remains `1`, preserves the Jacobian up to determinant
+   `1`, and shifts all pi-roots by the same common term.
+
+2. Source scaling to set the second tangent to `pi=gamma`.  With
+   `a=r2-r1 != 0`, scale `gamma` by a nonzero constant.  This is an
+   automorphism with nonzero determinant.  It rescales `J` by a nonzero scalar,
+   so `J=c gamma` stays of the same form with `c != 0`.  It changes no
+   exponents, gcds, or multiplicities.  The nonvanishing `a != 0` is forced by
+   the two-disc datum; if `a=0`, the `V2=3` split would not produce the two
+   tangent directions used by Definition 5.1 / Proposition 5.3.
+
+3. Approximate-root/Tschirnhausen choice.  The absence of the `h^4` term in
+   `Q=h^5+...` is the usual approximate-root normalization for the degree-4
+   root `h`; it is a coordinate choice in the approximate-root expansion, not a
+   deletion of target points.
+
+4. Target translations and shear.  The gauged chart applies
+   `Q -> Q - const(beta_5)`, then `P -> P - alpha_2 Q`, then
+   `P -> P - const(alpha_7)`.  Since `alpha_2` is scalar in the order chart,
+   this is a triangular target automorphism with determinant one, plus constant
+   translations.  It preserves `pi`-degrees `(28,20)`, monicity, and
+   `J(Q,P)` exactly.  In the reciprocal Laurent expansion, the shear contributes
+   first at exponent `-20`, divisible by `d2=4`, so it cannot create or remove
+   the first non-4-divisible tuple term.  Constant translations are invisible
+   to the infinity characteristic data.
+
+These checks address the FALLACY-v2 variable/ring-map and floor/attainment
+guards: no parameter-dependent leader is inverted, and the top-line
+localization is exactly the forced `a != 0` branch.
